WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.423
Windows Event Logs are one of the most important sources for forensic analysis.

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:15.948
The Security log can include authentication, account management, privilege use, policy changes, and other security-relevant activity depending on audit settings.

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:22.814
The System log can show service starts, driver events, shutdowns, and operating system issues.

00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:26.580
The Application log can contain application-specific events.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:33.446
PowerShell logs, when enabled, can provide useful evidence about script execution and command activity.

00:00:33.446 --> 00:00:39.870
Defender logs may show malware detections, remediation actions, configuration changes, and protection events.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:48.951
EDR products often add another layer of telemetry, such as process creation, network connections, file activity, and behavioral alerts.

00:00:48.951 --> 00:00:52.274
Each source has strengths and gaps.

00:00:52.274 --> 00:00:59.583
Event IDs are useful reference points, but Windows forensics is not only memorizing event numbers.

00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:08.665
The meaning of an event depends on audit policy, operating system version, domain configuration, endpoint controls, and surrounding activity.

00:01:08.665 --> 00:01:17.303
A logon event should be interpreted with the logon type, account, source, target system, timestamp, and related events.

00:01:17.303 --> 00:01:18.854
Retention matters.

00:01:18.854 --> 00:01:23.505
Logs may be overwritten, archived, cleared, forwarded, or unavailable.

00:01:23.505 --> 00:01:30.814
Missing or cleared logs are worth investigating, but they do not automatically prove malicious action.

00:01:30.814 --> 00:01:39.896
Analysts should compare local logs with centralized logging, domain controller records, EDR telemetry, cloud identity records, and other sources.

00:01:39.896 --> 00:01:45.876
Event logs are powerful when they are part of a larger timeline.
